--  What a script was invoked with.
--
--  `adash build.adash release --quiet` gives the script three things to read:
--  Argument_Count says how many, and Argument says which. Before they existed
--  the shell ran the script and discarded everything after its path, silently,
--  which is worse than refusing it.
--
--  Anything after the path belongs to the script, options included. A script's
--  own `-v` is the script's business and not the shell's, which is why the
--  shell stops reading options at the first name it sees.
--
--  This file is checked against a fixed output, so it is run with nothing.
--  Everything below is what a script does when it was given nothing, which is
--  the case a script has to handle first.

put_line (f"given {Argument_Count'Image}");

--  Reading past either end is the empty string rather than a failure, so a
--  script can ask whether it was given something without counting first.
if Argument (1) = "" then
   put_line ("no first argument");
end if;

--  Which makes a default expressible in the language, as it was for Env_Value.
function Positional (Position : Integer; Fallback : String) return String is
   Found : String := Argument (Position);
begin
   if Found = "" then
      return Fallback;
   end if;

   return Found;
end Positional;

put_line (Positional (1, "release"));

--  And the whole list, in order. Nothing is split and nothing is re-scanned:
--  an argument with a space in it is one argument, because a String here is a
--  value rather than source waiting to be interpreted again.
for Position in 1 .. Argument_Count loop
   put_line (f"{Position'Image}: {Argument (Position)}");
end loop;
